Reset, instead of accepting, a TCP connection that an app outside the VPN
pushes into the tun on Android, behind keqdroid's outside-vpn rule.

An app excluded from the VPN can still bind a socket to tun0 (SO_BINDTODEVICE
needs no rights since Linux 5.7). keqdroid writes a first rule, tagged
outside-vpn, that blocks connections the app names as outside the VPN (see
xray-android-uid-owner.patch and ConnectionOwnerServer.kt). But the stack
completes the TCP handshake before routing runs (proxy/tun/stack.go, gVisor
before xray 26.10.21), so such an app saw a connection that opened and then
closed: proof that the tun answers.

proxy/tun now asks the router for that one rule before the handshake
(Router.MatchRuleTag applies only the rule with the given tag, nothing above
it) and answers a match with a reset. No outside-vpn rule in the config means
no lookup and no change (Router.HasRuleTag). The owner lookup goes through the
finder's two second cache, so the routing that follows does not ask the app
again. UDP has no handshake and keeps being dropped by the rule itself.

The stack takes a SYN in the loop that reads the tun, and the lookup asks the
app over a socket: done there, it would hold up every packet. The SYN waits in
a goroutine of its own, as gVisor's forwarder ran its handler, and the same
SYN sent again meanwhile is dropped. Without the rule a SYN is answered in the
loop as upstream does.

Android only (owner_guard_android.go); other platforms get a stub.

Built by tool/build_xray_cores.ps1. Checked against xray-core v26.11.9.

